static lstr L2161 = {-1, 5, "E0923"};
static lstr L2162 = {-1, 125, "Narra has no classes. Data goes in a struct and behavior in functions of the module.\012Fix: `class A {}` becomes `struct A {}`."};
static lstr L2163 = {-1, 5, "E0924"};
static lstr L2164 = {-1, 125, "Narra has no interfaces. Use a struct for a data shape and a trait for behavior.\012Fix: `interface A {}` becomes `struct A {}`."};
static lstr L2165 = {-1, 5, "E0925"};
static lstr L2166 = {-1, 153, "Narra has no `new`. A struct is built with a literal, other values with a function call.\012Fix: `new A()` becomes `A {}` when A is a struct without fields."};
static lstr L2167 = {-1, 5, "E0926"};
static lstr L2168 = {-1, 120, "Narra has no exceptions. A function that can fail returns `Result<T, E>`; the caller checks it with `is Err` or `match`."};
static lstr L2169 = {-1, 5, "E0927"};
static lstr L2170 = {-1, 124, "Narra has no `throw`. Return `Err(value)` from a function that returns a Result.\012Fix: `throw \"x\"` becomes `return Err(\"x\")`."};
static lstr L2171 = {-1, 5, "E0928"};
static lstr L2172 = {-1, 62, "Narra has no `this`. A function gets the value as a parameter."};
static lstr L2173 = {-1, 5, "E0930"};
static lstr L2174 = {-1, 583, "Narra has no function declarations inside a function. Declare the function at module level as a private function and pass the enclosing values as arguments.\012There is no automatic fix: moving it changes the meaning when its body reads a name that a local shadows, and breaks helpers that use the enclosing type parameters.\012For a helper that captures, bind an arrow function: `const helper = (x: int): int => x + offset`.\012Example: `function run() { function add(a: int): int { return a + 1 } }` becomes `function add(a: int): int { return a + 1 }` followed by `function run() { ... }`."};
static lstr L2175 = {-1, 5, "E0991"};
static lstr L2176 = {-1, 493, "A `@target` function exists only on its target, so a use must sit where only that target compiles it: inside an `if` on `Build.os` or `Build.arch` that the other targets drop, or inside a `@target` function with the same or a narrower target. An ordinary `if`, a `match` and an early `return` do not count.\012`narrac check` checks the host only; `narrac check --all-targets` checks every supported target.\012Example: `print(linuxName())` becomes `if (Build.os == Os.Linux) { print(linuxName()) }`."};
static lstr L2177 = {-1, 5, "E0992"};
static lstr L2178 = {-1, 440, "A constant type parameter is written `const N: int` in the type parameters of a function or a struct. Its kind is `int`; enums, type aliases and other integer kinds do not take one. A type argument is a type, an integer literal or a constant parameter in scope; it never computes.\012Example: `Vector<N + 1>` is rejected; pass the length as its own constant parameter, `function grow<const N: int, const M: int>(vector: Vector<N>): Vector<M>`."};
static lstr L2179 = {-1, 5, "E0993"};
static lstr L2180 = {-1, 362, "A constant parameter takes an integer literal or a constant parameter in scope, and a type parameter takes a type; the two do not mix. A local, a type or a top-level constant cannot stand for a constant parameter, and a constant cannot stand for a type.\012Example: `Vector<count>` becomes `Vector<3>`, and `value: N` becomes `value: int` with `N` read in the body."};
static lstr L2181 = {-1, 5, "E0994"};
static lstr L2182 = {-1, 527, "Indexing a FixedArray has no runtime check, so every index is proven at compile time. A constant index must be from 0 to N - 1. Any other index must be a plain name that a check puts in that range: an `if` or `while` condition such as `i >= 0 && i < 4`, the condition of `for (let i = 0; i < N; i++)`, or `for (const i of Ranges.upTo(N))`. An assignment to the name ends what the check proved. `FixedArrays.get(values, i)` returns an Option for any index.\012Example: `values[i]` becomes `if (i >= 0 && i < 4) { use(values[i]) }`."};
static lstr L2183 = {-1, 5, "E0995"};
static lstr L2184 = {-1, 366, "`FixedArray<T, N>` takes an element type and a length. The length is an integer literal or a constant type parameter, and never negative. One FixedArray value takes at most 1048576 bytes (1 MiB); use an Array for more. A literal builds a FixedArray when the expected type is one, and gives every element.\012Example: `const point: FixedArray<f64, 3> = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]`."};
static lstr L2185 = {-1, 5, "E0996"};
static lstr L2186 = {-1, 354, "A call of a std/ptr function or of a function with the unsafe effect may break memory safety, so it is allowed only inside an `unsafe { ... }` block. The function that holds the block gets the unsafe effect, unless it is marked `@trusted`.\012Example: `const value = Ptr.read(items)` becomes `let value = 0` followed by `unsafe { value = Ptr.read(items) }`."};
static lstr L2187 = {-1, 5, "E0997"};
static lstr L2188 = {-1, 328, "`@trusted` states that a function's `unsafe` blocks were checked by hand, and stops the unsafe effect there: callers need no `unsafe` block. A function without an `unsafe` block has nothing to trust, and a `@trusted` function does not declare `uses unsafe`.\012Example: remove `@trusted` from a function that has no `unsafe` block."};
static lstr L2189 = {-1, 5, "E0998"};
static lstr L2190 = {-1, 457, "A `Ptr<T>` is a raw pointer with no reference count. It may point at numbers, `bool`, `char`, another `Ptr` and structs whose fields are all such values, because a pointer to a string, array, map, box, enum or closure would bypass reference counting. The std/ptr functions take a `Ptr`; `Ptr.alloc`, `Ptr.null` and `Ptr.cast` take the element type from where the result goes.\012Example: `Ptr<string>` is rejected; `const items: Ptr<int> = Ptr.alloc(4)` works."};
static lstr L2191 = {-1, 5, "E1000"};
static lstr L2192 = {-1, 431, "C functions are declared in a block that names their library: `extern { function sqrt(value: f64): f64 } from \"m\"`. A member is a signature with no type parameters, no `uses` and no body; it has the unsafe effect. Only `@extern(\"name\")` may stand before a member. The libraries come from `from`, so `[link]` in narra.toml takes only `search`.\012Example: `[link] libraries = [\"m\"]` is removed; write `from \"m\"` after the extern block."};
static lstr L2193 = {-1, 5, "E1001"};
static lstr L2194 = {-1, 485, "A C function is linked by its C name, the member's name or the one `@extern` gives. That name must be a C identifier and must not be one the compiler writes itself: `main`, names starting with `narra_` or `lstr_`, string literal names such as `L1`, and the generated names of the program's functions and types, alone or with a helper's operation before or a suffix after (`release_E_Option_e088c655`).\012Example: `@extern(\"narra_alloc\")` is rejected; call the C function by its own name."};
static lstr L2195 = {-1, 5, "E1002"};
static lstr L2196 = {-1, 326, "A C function takes and returns values C can pass: the number types, `bool` and `Ptr<T>`, and may return nothing. Strings, arrays and other counted values have no C form; pass a `Ptr<u8>`, for a string `CStrings.pointer` of a CString.\012Example: `function strlen(text: string): u64` becomes `function strlen(text: Ptr<u8>): u64`."};
static lstr L2197 = {-1, 5, "E1003"};
static lstr L2198 = {-1, 302, "`typeof` of a constant needs the constant's type, and a constant without a written type has its type only after its value is checked. In a declaration of the same module (a signature, a field, an alias) that type is not known yet, so write it.\012Example: `const limit = 3` becomes `const limit: int = 3`."};
static lstr L2199 = {-1, 5, "E1004"};
static lstr L2200 = {-1, 205, "The compiler tracks a position as a line and a column below 1048576 each. A token past either limit is rejected, since it would share a record with another position. Split the file, or break the long line."};
static lstr L2201 = {-1, 13, "unsupported: "};
